[{:db/id "q-339953/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "44c44301-edd4-5f24-91a6-6af2cc80d3bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339953/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3f129fe-fa2b-5846-b334-35af1aebc21d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339953/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-339953/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339953/field-2/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "39b8a916-a195-5e48-b50c-510ef6cc92a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339953/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "cdadc70f-39b7-5af5-842d-f7c154d28196" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339953/field-2/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-339953/field-2/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339953" :question/id #uuid "7aa3567c-9137-58ac-b2ca-e555c0991f8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339953" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x-5}{{x}^{2}-4x-21}$ are\n\n$x=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x-5}{{x}^{2}-4x-21} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x-5}{(x-7)(x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-7)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\\,x=7\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are $x=-3$ and $x=7,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/56b3d93afca64c37cd48013702f2317f41935e85d5b795c34f31a76927852d44.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339953/field-1" "q-339953/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339953"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339951/field-1/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer/id #uuid "8d5db644-b43e-5270-a3a7-bea4f3d93efc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339951/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "9778a7b0-25bf-57c3-8a51-1f1186c366c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339951/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6be7f9bc-b435-5197-9995-11c6c7f803ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339951/field-1/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer-field/choices ["q-339951/field-1/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" "q-339951/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339951/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "ec1ccfc4-87aa-5658-a4e9-e4fd91312f20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339951/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1b0f90bd-f8aa-574a-a366-b1e339f3485a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339951/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-339951/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339951" :question/id #uuid "d94d162d-c46c-566a-8df0-7c2509808895" :question/math-academy-id "q-339951" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+5x-14}$ are\n\n$x=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+5x-14} \\\\ y & =\\frac{8}{(x-2)(x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+7)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-7,\\,x=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are $x=-7$ and $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/069b968e405a1302f67a085da7aff92979c95d4fca423aad3885ca6267d8cc34.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339951/field-1" "q-339951/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339951"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339947/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "16ad7813-4270-59b7-9380-442a2c52b5e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339947/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "66a398d7-d681-5cb3-9d82-8663743fac65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339947/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-339947/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339947/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "b4c20995-f4be-5507-9906-2fc4d1f236c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339947/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "0be7f74c-4752-510d-8220-6e02cb9ba388" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339947/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-339947/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339947" :question/id #uuid "abc06c45-2df3-5a27-9dad-f25ba334ab5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339947" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9-{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9-{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{(3-x)(3+x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(3-x)(3+x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,3\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=-3$ and $x=3$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/ead2334ba9281edbe8545c4d18b8e64bb72e28915d98674fe68364dfdc4e0a4e.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339947/field-1" "q-339947/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339947"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47854/selection/da6ec38f87287c48f97b7f5538a006b7adbc63bec0be5e152accc6b9f3071e1b" :answer/id #uuid "d50dd59c-e9e5-56f4-a0fb-4e4cfb60de89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47854/selection/4e6eeef5aa33ee4a1d2fff0c2887be5f32fe5006030d9cd9500bd4b039270d64" :answer/id #uuid "98948942-15fd-517d-810b-d0c9bd4b0be8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47854/selection/3bf490a97b4b01d003a4d517d1cd8f80a0e11ffcaac2332bbdf3cd4e7da1ce42" :answer/id #uuid "96b3dec0-1f05-5c68-9512-bdebc2a567fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47854/selection/c3fa3328ef1bf03387c5b9ecfc46ff44cb485f68e0386889e9160fbb38c65131" :answer/id #uuid "b655f1ec-64ce-56c1-83c3-6c678e031c46" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There are no vertical asymptotes." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47854/selection/942405f500b03a053b52160d81933044177bc757c5f35618476b53dffbbccf08" :answer/id #uuid "de41d696-daab-5b8e-b788-fc554e5810f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47854/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4018887a-b7dc-5e26-88f3-652e88837dce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-47854/selection/3bf490a97b4b01d003a4d517d1cd8f80a0e11ffcaac2332bbdf3cd4e7da1ce42" :answer-field/choices ["q-47854/selection/da6ec38f87287c48f97b7f5538a006b7adbc63bec0be5e152accc6b9f3071e1b" "q-47854/selection/4e6eeef5aa33ee4a1d2fff0c2887be5f32fe5006030d9cd9500bd4b039270d64" "q-47854/selection/3bf490a97b4b01d003a4d517d1cd8f80a0e11ffcaac2332bbdf3cd4e7da1ce42" "q-47854/selection/c3fa3328ef1bf03387c5b9ecfc46ff44cb485f68e0386889e9160fbb38c65131" "q-47854/selection/942405f500b03a053b52160d81933044177bc757c5f35618476b53dffbbccf08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47854" :question/id #uuid "b2c4ac97-8cea-5552-8956-b9b9e4733346" :question/math-academy-id "q-47854" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\frac{5}{x-\\sqrt{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "There are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-\\sqrt{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is the only vertical asymptote of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/09c19a6049d77a2b57c093ca714627a453c99bf52af27ae656ad1f4e779d1866.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-47854/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47854"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47853/selection/7ae3aae3c60e26ea57a80d799f6557d26718d52ce247d1ab2c3ae9405150de03" :answer/id #uuid "cbc32ec7-8a46-508f-87ef-c55ea86bd785" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=-6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47853/selection/5ae5bd5f0ff87d7a7d38712354fc7e51cfcc2c56cec99dca9e7c6a359da02666" :answer/id #uuid "85b5a260-ac60-52ea-a55c-34f8f616a856" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47853/selection/6b2872c7302e3457b03c111c6d7f1270f6effb76880a152846526c5d2b9796ba" :answer/id #uuid "155cbef5-d4bf-5d19-9119-2b6c4f607dac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47853/selection/da9bc9ddf9c4743dae0c84081e85ef019e2fc10efb714e93a58af6259dfb24c1" :answer/id #uuid "92fffbe3-63fa-51f0-954e-96df25de2048" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=-2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47853/selection/c3fa3328ef1bf03387c5b9ecfc46ff44cb485f68e0386889e9160fbb38c65131" :answer/id #uuid "370f7a98-302d-5a74-8f4d-c2d5a1d408df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There are no vertical asymptotes." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47853/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f28c8f37-1cb0-51be-b67f-af017d0511c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-47853/selection/6b2872c7302e3457b03c111c6d7f1270f6effb76880a152846526c5d2b9796ba" :answer-field/choices ["q-47853/selection/7ae3aae3c60e26ea57a80d799f6557d26718d52ce247d1ab2c3ae9405150de03" "q-47853/selection/5ae5bd5f0ff87d7a7d38712354fc7e51cfcc2c56cec99dca9e7c6a359da02666" "q-47853/selection/6b2872c7302e3457b03c111c6d7f1270f6effb76880a152846526c5d2b9796ba" "q-47853/selection/da9bc9ddf9c4743dae0c84081e85ef019e2fc10efb714e93a58af6259dfb24c1" "q-47853/selection/c3fa3328ef1bf03387c5b9ecfc46ff44cb485f68e0386889e9160fbb38c65131"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47853" :question/id #uuid "53858ba9-755e-54c6-83a7-797ed7120dc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47853" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x-6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-6)(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-6)(x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1,6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=6$ and $x=-1$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/b3d4116e1157d2f9b5414a015965b371982663518d04631810f19d8be4a7de93.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-47853/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47853"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339971/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "20d3780f-cd43-5899-9334-a4e849304d08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339971/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a4d29871-5f53-514f-86eb-12c448d859ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339971/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "efe1a369-1e4d-591d-9265-7d6eb297aa60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339971/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-339971/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-339971/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339971" :question/id #uuid "469b9897-9d0c-516a-93de-61de2ed705a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-339971" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{{x}^{2}-4x}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{{x}^{2}-4x} & =\\frac{2x(x+4)}{x(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{x}(x+4)}{\\cancel{x}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+4)}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339971/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80b8388b-ae0b-5ea6-9efc-fed1a074f463"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339971"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37066"] :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-7x+12}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-7x+12} & =\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-4x-3x+12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{x(x-4)-3(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{(x-4)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{(x-3)}}{(x-4)\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-339976/field-1/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "9643f39a-af86-5403-bd4b-40bed5569f7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339976/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "ff7869e3-8f98-5186-962f-b4b9d4d83767" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339976/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7136970-f63a-5d20-948b-d530596f642f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339976/field-1/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-339976/field-1/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-339976/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339976" :question/id #uuid "ff284a84-3518-5c5c-aee6-4a46c4366526" :question/math-academy-id "q-339976" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-8}{{x}^{2}+7x+10}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-8}{{x}^{2}+7x+10} & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-4)}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{2}^{2})}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)(x+2)}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{(x+5)\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+5=0\\,⟹\\,x=-5\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339976/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80b8388b-ae0b-5ea6-9efc-fed1a074f463"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339976"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339960/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "a5660f26-e5fd-5046-a763-c39d7e6a2f22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339960/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c6390b-6c28-57b3-a1b9-b88e20a4f1aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339960/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-339960/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339960" :question/id #uuid "399f0d67-1eb9-57db-a89e-719174076197" :question/math-academy-id "q-339960" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{4x-8}{2{x}^{2}+2x-12}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x-8}{2{x}^{2}+2x-12} & =\\frac{4(x-2)}{2(x+3)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{(x-2)}}{2(x+3)\\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339960/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80b8388b-ae0b-5ea6-9efc-fed1a074f463"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339960"] :db/ensure [